                <pb facs="1191" n="1079" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg1079"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.628/136: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Neville</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-11-29T17:00:00-05:00">November 29, 1935—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>186. Your 208, November 12, 5 p.m. On November 27, the Department discussed the
                    matter orally with the Japanese Ambassador,<anchor n="66" xml:id="d1035-f1"/>
                    who stated that he would lay before his Government our view in regard to the
                    desirability of there being concluded a convention.</p>
                <p>Please take no further action pending the receipt of the instruction which the
                    Department will expect to send you as soon as a reply will have been made by the
                    Japanese Ambassador.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1035-f1" n="66" xml:id="d1035-f1-content">
                <p>See section II of memorandum dated November 27, <ref target="#pg1020">p.
                        1020</ref>.</p>
            </note>
